    "text": "We use the negative learned value function as the terminal cost so that _qf_ ( **x** _NT_ ) = _−V_<sup>**_ψϕ_**</sup> ( **x** _NT ,_ **_ℓ_**<sup>�</sup> _NT_ ). In this way, we approximate the infinite horizon trajectory cost given the current LiDAR measurement. We apply the additional constraint that the learned value function improves from the current state to the final state of the trajectory: \n\n\n\n\n\nDuring each planning interval, PAC-NMPC optimizes and returns policy hyperparameters, **_ν_**<sup>_∗_</sup> , a PAC bound on the expected cost, _Jα_<sup>+(</sup><sup>**_ν_**</sup><sup>_∗_),andaPACboundontheprobability</sup> of constraint violation, _Cα_<sup>+(</sup><sup>**_ν_**</sup><sup>_∗_).Theseboundsserveasper-</sup> formance and safety guarantees for the policy; the probability of violating the obstacle constraint or worsening the learned value function, given the current LiDAR measurement, will be less than _Cα_<sup>+(</sup><sup>**_ν_**)withaprobabilityof1</sup><sup>_−δ_(eq.III-A).</sup> \n\nV. SIMULATION EXPERIMENTS \n\n# _A. Experimental Setup_ \n\nWe simulate a stochastic bicycle model with acceleration and steering rate inputs given as \n\n\n\nHere **x** _t_ = [ _px py θ v δs_ ]<sup>_T_</sup> is the state vector, **u** _t_ = [˙ _v δ_<sup>˙</sup> _s_ ]<sup>_T_</sup> is the control vector, _l_ = 0 _._ 33 _m_ is the wheelbase, **_ω_** is Gaussian noise, and **Γ** = _diag_ (�4e<sup>_−_4</sup> _,_ 4e<sup>_−_4</sup> _,_ 1 _._ 1e<sup>_−_2</sup> _,_ 1e<sup>_−_1</sup> _,_ 5 _._ 6e<sup>_−_3�</sup> ) is the process covariance, which was fit from hardware data. We limit the acceleration, _v_ ˙ _∈_ [ _−_ 1 _.,_ 1]<sup>_<u>m</u>_</sup> _s_<sup>,thesteeringrate,</sup> _δ_ ˙ _s ∈_ [ _−_ 1 _,_ 1]<sup>_<u>rad</u>_</sup> _s_<sup>, and the steering angle,</sup><sup>_δs∈_[</sup><sup>_−_0</sup><sup>_._4</sup><sup>_,_0</sup><sup>_._4]</sup><sup>_rad_.</sup> We use a quadratic state cost _q_ ( **x** _,_ **u** ) = ( **x** _−_ **x** _G_ )<sup>_T_</sup> _Q_ ( **x** _−_ **x** _G_ ) where _Q_ = _diag_ ([1e<sup>_−_2</sup> 1e<sup>_−_2</sup> 0 0 0]), and a velocity constraint such that _v ∈_ [ _−_ 1 _,_ 3]<sup>_<u>m</u>_</sup> _s_<sup>.</sup> \n\nOur approach considers not only uncertainty in the dynamics, but also in the learned actor and critic networks. For every trajectory sampled by PAC-NMPC, we also sample dropout masks for the actor and critic networks [53] before evaluating the cost-to-go at the trajectory’s terminal state. Since our approach already requires multiple forward passes through the networks, one for each trajectory, Monte Carlo dropout does not add additional inference time. This allows us to incorporate value function uncertainty into the PAC bound to provide more accurate performance guarantees. \n\nWe compared our approach against four baselines across two simulation environment distributions. The actor and critic networks were trained for each environment. First, we evaluated PAC-NMPC with a quadratic terminal cost, _qf_ ( **x** _NT_ ) = ( **x** _NT −_ **x** _G_ )<sup>_T_</sup> _Qf_ ( **x** _NT −_ **x** _G_ ) where _Qf_ = _diag_ ([1 1 0 0 0]). Second, we used the LiDAR measurements to continuously build and maintain an occupancy grid of the environment. Each planning iteration, we ran a naive A* search algorithm on the grid to find the shortest path to the \n\n\n\nFig. 2. Simulated cluttered environments. Percentage of trials in which the system reached the goal, didn’t reach the goal, and violated constraints. \n\n\n\nFig. 3. Simulated cluttered environments. Testing environment where PACNMPC fails when only using a quadratic terminal cost. \n\ngoal. A receding horizon goal, **x** _A_ , was selected at distance of _vmax · Nt ·_ ∆ _t_ along the path, and was used to form a quadratic terminal cost, _qf_ ( **x** _NT_ ) = ( **x** _NT −_ **x** _A_ )<sup>_T_</sup> _Qf_ ( **x** _NT −_ **x** _A_ ). Third, we compared directly against the actor policy, **_π_**<sup>**_ϕ_**</sup> ( **s** ). Fourth, instead of using PAC-NMPC, we optimized trajectories with MPPI [54], while still using the learned value function as a terminal trajectory cost. \n\nWhen running PAC-NMPC, we optimize a 12 timestep trajectory with ∆ _t_ = 0 _._ 1 sec at a replanning period of _H_ = 0 _._ 2 sec, and we interpolate the feedback policies to 50Hz. We set PAC-NMPC parameters as _L_ = 5, _M_ = 1024, _δ_ = 0 _._ 05, and normalize the trajectory costs before optimization to achieve tighter PAC bounds. We evaluated PAC-NMPC with several _γ_ values and selected the best value for each environment; _γ_ = 2 in the cluttered environments and _γ_ = 4 in the concave trap environments. When running MPPI, we similarly normalized the sampled trajectory costs and summed them with the constraints using the same _γ_ values. We used the same number of timesteps, ∆ _t_ , _H_ , number of sampled trajectories, a temperature of _γt_ = 0 _._ 35, and a sampling variance of Σ _ϵ_ = 0 _._ 01. We allowed MPPI to run for as many iterations as possible in the replanning period and interpolated the resulting trajectories to 50Hz. \n\nWe trained the actor and critic in simulation with the \n\n"
